  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: An anticyclone aloft will move\nsoutheastward into the South Central United States this \nafternoon. This will pull monsoonal moisture northward into most\nof Northern and Central Nevada, resulting in scattered \nthunderstorms this afternoon and this evening. Elko may break its\nall time record for precipitable water this afternoon. There is a\nslight risk of excessive rainfall in most of Eastern Nevada today.\nIn addition, there is a Flash Flood Watch in effect for most of \nNorthern and Central Nevada today. Here are thunderstorm \nprobabilities for select locations in Eastern Nevada on Tuesday, \nJuly 21st, 2026:\n\nEureka - 57%\nLund - 50%\nRuby Lake - 50%\nEly - 49%\nAustin - 48%\nCurrie - 47%\nTonopah - 44%\nWells - 43%\nGreat Basin National Park - 43%\nElko - 42%\n\nMonsoonal moisture will remain in place over most of Northern and\nCentral Nevada Wednesday, setting up another period of inclement\nweather Wednesday afternoon and Wednesday evening, although not as\nactive as today. The aforementioned upper-level area of high\npressure will expand northwestward Thursday through Monday. As a\nresult, storm chances will dwindle Thursday through Sunday, before\nending by Monday of next week.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \nHigh forecast confidence regarding historically high atmospheric\nmoisture content today. High forecast confidence in a Flash Flood\nWatch for most of Northern and Central Nevada today. High forecast \nconfidence regarding a few strong thunderstorms throughout \nNorthern and Central Nevada each afternoon and evening today and \nWednesday. Moderate forecast confidence in decreasing rain chances\nThursday through Sunday. No deviations from the NBM model output.",
